#
# init.recovery.sakura_diag.rc - early-boot diagnostic collector (sakura)
#
# WHY A SERVICE AND NOT `exec`
# ----------------------------
# init's `exec` builtin blocks the trigger until the command exits. A boot
# logger must never delay the boot sequence, so it is declared as a `oneshot`
# service:
# init forks and supervises it asynchronously, and `oneshot` means a completed
# or crashed run is never restarted in a loop.
#
# ORDERING (measured against the pinned etc/init.rc)
# ------------------------------------------------
# The device rc is imported at the top of init.rc, so its `on late-init` action
# is queued BEFORE init.rc's own late-init action. That action is what triggers
# `boot`, and `on boot` runs `class_start default`, which starts the GUI
# (`service recovery`, from init.recovery.service22.rc). Starting here therefore
# guarantees the first snapshot and its `setprop` happen before gui_init(),
# while /tmp is already mounted by `on init` and logd is running.
#
# QSEECom/Keymaster/Gatekeeper start later (`on boot`); the bounded watch loop
# re-samples `init.svc.*` every few seconds, so their real results are captured
# without blocking boot.
#
# The collector itself is bounded (default 90 s of sampling) and writes only to
# tmpfs plus an already-writable /cache. See documentation/DIAGNOSTICS.md.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later
#

service sakura_diag /system/bin/sakura-diag boot
    class core
    user root
    group root system
    seclabel u:r:recovery:s0
    oneshot
    disabled

on late-init
    # PRODUCT_COPY_FILES preserves the executable bit, but init refuses to exec
    # a non-executable file and the failure is easy to miss in a log. Make the
    # mode explicit and cheap.
    chmod 0755 /system/bin/sakura-diag
    mkdir /tmp/sakura-diag 0750 root system
    start sakura_diag
